; FESTITEMS.SCR — permanent definitions for Fall Festival / Halloween keepsakes
; =============================================================================
; Loaded from LEGENDS.CFG (unlike FALLFEST.SCR / HALLOWEEN2026.SCR, which are
; hot-loaded only for the festival). Everything players can buy or earn at the
; festival is defined here, so those items keep their names, descriptions and
; scripts after the event files are removed and the server restarts.
; Room fixtures, scenery, monsters and story logic stay in the event files.
; See original/HALLOWEEN_RUNBOOK.md.
;
; ID ranges (the 93xx block is reserved for Halloween / Fall Festival):
;   NOUNDEF 9300-9303, 9350-9351    ADJDEF 9300-9311, 9350-9356
;   INUMBER 9302-9306, 9320-9331, 9344-9346 (Last Lantern of Fayd, 2026)
;           9350-9357 (Fall Festival goods)
; =============================================================================

; ── Vocabulary ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NOUNDEF 9300 gravestone
NOUNDEF 9301 gravestones
NOUNDEF 9302 strip
NOUNDEF 9303 prybar
ADJDEF 9300 sunrise
ADJDEF 9301 teardrop
ADJDEF 9302 wheatsheaf
ADJDEF 9303 hammer
ADJDEF 9304 birdcage
ADJDEF 9305 hound
ADJDEF 9306 handprint
ADJDEF 9307 leafless
ADJDEF 9308 obelisk
ADJDEF 9309 funeral
ADJDEF 9310 maytan
ADJDEF 9311 ironbound

NOUNDEF 9350 campfire
NOUNDEF 9351 cider
ADJDEF 9350 treasure
ADJDEF 9351 spiderweb
ADJDEF 9352 festival
ADJDEF 9353 spiced
ADJDEF 9354 caramel
ADJDEF 9355 trestle
ADJDEF 9356 crackling

; ── Festival goods ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

; Fall Festival shirt (Tailor's Shop). Shirt slot/flags copied from the plain
; shirt in ITEM1.SCR. Edit the tagline each festival year.
INUMBER 9350
NAME 183
ADJECTIVE 9352
MISC
WORN_TORSO2
WEIGHT 1
VOLUME 2
SUBSTANCE CLOTH
*DESCRIPTION_START EXAMINE
Printed across the front of this soft shirt is a grinning jack-o'-lantern above the words "FAYD FALL FESTIVAL" in jagged letters. Beneath them, in smaller print, it reads: "I braved the Old Fayd Cemetery and all I got was this shirt."
*DESCRIPTION_END
;
; Festival mask (Tailor's Shop). Sold as pumpkin/skull/ghostly/black via the
; store adjective. WORN_DON like the existing masks.
INUMBER 9357
NAME 522
MISC
WORN_DON
WEIGHT 1
VOLUME 1
SUBSTANCE CLOTH
*DESCRIPTION_START EXAMINE
A festival mask of stiffened, painted cloth with a ribbon to tie it on. The eyeholes are just a little too large, which makes the wearer look permanently startled.
*DESCRIPTION_END
;
; Sugar skull (Bakery).
INUMBER 9351
NAME 188
ADJECTIVE 1070
FOOD
WEIGHT 1
VOLUME 1
PARAMETER1 3
SUBSTANCE INSUBSTANTIAL
*DESCRIPTION_START EXAMINE
A fist-sized skull of pressed sugar, decorated with swirls of bright icing: flowers around the eye sockets, a row of neat little teeth, and a tiny heart on the forehead.
*DESCRIPTION_END
IFVERB EAT -1
  ECHO PLAYER The sugar skull crunches and then melts on your tongue, sweet enough to make your teeth ache.
ENDIF
;
; Caramel apple (Bakery).
INUMBER 9352
NAME 704
ADJECTIVE 9354
FOOD
WEIGHT 1
VOLUME 1
PARAMETER1 5
*DESCRIPTION_START EXAMINE
A crisp red apple on a stick, dipped in thick, glossy caramel and rolled in crushed nuts.
*DESCRIPTION_END
IFVERB EAT -1
  ECHO PLAYER The caramel sticks to your teeth, the apple crunches, and for a moment all is right with the world.
  ECHO OTHERS %N happily chews on a caramel apple, getting caramel all over %h chin.
ENDIF
;
; Spiced cider (Bakery, and free at the festival lot's table).
INUMBER 9353
NAME 9351
ADJECTIVE 9353
LIQUID
ARTICLE SOME
PARAMETER1 4
WEIGHT 1
VOLUME 1
IFVERB DRINK -1
  ECHO PLAYER The hot cider tastes of apples, cinnamon and clove, and warms you all the way down to your toes.
ENDIF
;
; Pumpkin bread (Bakery, and free at the festival lot's table).
INUMBER 9354
NAME 345
ADJECTIVE 971
FOOD
ARTICLE SOME
WEIGHT 1
VOLUME 1
PARAMETER1 5
IFVERB EAT -1
  ECHO PLAYER The pumpkin bread is moist and warm, rich with nutmeg and brown sugar.
ENDIF
;
; Plastic spider ring (Randolphs, and a treasure-balloon prize). One fixed
; adjective only — with two ("plastic spider ring") the target matcher can't take
; "black spider ring". Randolphs sells it by color ("a black spider ring"); the
; balloon prize is "a black plastic spider ring" (ADJ1 black, ADJ2 plastic).
INUMBER 9355
NAME 167
ADJECTIVE 545
MISC
WORN_RING
WEIGHT 1
VOLUME 1
*DESCRIPTION_START EXAMINE
A cheap, bendable plastic ring topped with a fat little spider whose eight legs wobble whenever you move your hand. It is utterly tasteless, and you love it.
*DESCRIPTION_END
IFPREVERB TAP -1
  CLEARVERB
  ECHO PLAYER You flick the spider on your ring and watch its legs wobble.
  ECHO OTHERS %N flicks the plastic spider on %h ring, making its legs wobble.
ENDIF
;
; Spiderweb earcuff (Randolphs, and a treasure-balloon prize).
INUMBER 9356
NAME 80
ADJECTIVE 9351
MISC
WORN_EAR
WEIGHT 1
VOLUME 1
SUBSTANCE SOFTMETAL
*DESCRIPTION_START EXAMINE
Fine wire has been worked into a delicate spiderweb that climbs the curve of the ear. A tiny bead of polished jet sits in the web where the spider ought to be.
*DESCRIPTION_END
;

; ── The Last Lantern of Fayd (2026): clue keepsakes ──────────────────────────

; The token left in the ashes: "an ancient obelisk token" (ADJ1=7 ancient).
INUMBER 9302
NAME 1031
ADJECTIVE 9308
MISC
WEIGHT 1
VOLUME 1
SUBSTANCE SOFTMETAL
*DESCRIPTION_START EXAMINE
An ancient grave token of blackened bronze, still faintly warm from the ashes. Stamped into its face is the shape of a broken obelisk.
*DESCRIPTION_END
;
; Clue 1: the copper strip from the skull.
INUMBER 9303
NAME 9302
ADJECTIVE 68
MISC
WEIGHT 1
VOLUME 1
SUBSTANCE SOFTMETAL
*DESCRIPTION_START EXAMINE
A thin strip of copper, green with age, stamped with the image of a long, feathery leaf -- unmistakably the leaf of a Maytan tree. Words are inscribed along its length.
*DESCRIPTION_END
IFPREVERB READ -1
  CLEARVERB
  ECHO PLAYER Inscribed along the copper strip, beneath the Maytan leaf:
  ECHO PLAYER "Where grey leaves brush the names of the forgotten,
  ECHO PLAYER  seek the roots that drank the keeper's blood."
  ECHO OTHERS %N reads the words inscribed on a thin copper strip.
ENDIF
;
; Clue 2: the brass key from the Maytan roots ("a tarnished brass key").
INUMBER 9304
NAME 119
ADJECTIVE 32
MISC
WEIGHT 1
VOLUME 1
SUBSTANCE SOFTMETAL
*DESCRIPTION_START EXAMINE
A heavy, old-fashioned key of tarnished brass. The rotted remains of a leather cord are still knotted through its bow, as if someone once wore it around his neck.
*DESCRIPTION_END
;
; Clue 3: the blackened parchment from the caretaker's compartment.
INUMBER 9305
NAME 1401
ADJECTIVE 404
MISC
WEIGHT 1
VOLUME 1
*DESCRIPTION_START EXAMINE
A brittle piece of parchment, blackened at the edges as if by fire. Beneath a few lines of cramped writing is a crude drawing of a stone arch -- the entrance of a mausoleum on a small hill.
*DESCRIPTION_END
IFPREVERB READ -1
  CLEARVERB
  ECHO PLAYER In a cramped, careful hand, the blackened parchment reads:
  ECHO PLAYER "I buried no treasure among the dead. From the hill I watched them --
  ECHO PLAYER  and beneath the watcher, I hid what remained."
  ECHO PLAYER Below the words is a crude drawing of the stone arch of a mausoleum.
  ECHO OTHERS %N reads a piece of blackened parchment.
ENDIF
;
; The rusty prybar leaning against the caretaker's house (room 307).
INUMBER 9306
NAME 9303
ADJECTIVE 272
MISC
WEIGHT 3
VOLUME 4
SUBSTANCE HARDMETAL
*DESCRIPTION_START EXAMINE
A length of rusted iron, flattened and bent at one end. Good for prying things up that were meant to stay down.
*DESCRIPTION_END
;

